Check the address of a, i and c before reading their text.
The insertion point of a and i was checked only by insertLine, once the
first line of text had been read. An out-of-range address was reported
only after that line, which was lost, and the lines typed after it were
taken as commands, so text such as "1d" or "w" was executed. c had the
same problem and worse: deleteLines rejected a bad range, but c went on
to read the text and insert it before the first address. Check the
insertion point in addLines before reading anything, and the range of c
before deleting, so that the command fails at once in command mode, as
it does in other implementations of ed.

---
 editors/ed.c |    8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

--- a/editors/ed.c
+++ b/editors/ed.c
@@ -363,6 +363,12 @@
 	int len;
 	char buf[USERSIZE + 1];
 
+	/* Check before reading any text, so that the error shows at once */
+	if ((num < 1) || (num > lastNum + 1)) {
+		bb_simple_error_msg("inserting at bad line number");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	while (1) {
 		/* Returns:
 		 * -1 on read errors or EOF, or on bare Ctrl-D.
@@ -848,6 +854,8 @@
 			break;
 
 		case 'c':
+			if (bad_nums(num1, num2, "change"))
+				break;
 			deleteLines(num1, num2);
 			addLines(num1);
 			break;
